Subject: [PATCH v5 00/13] KVM: arm64: FEAT_HDBSS-based dirty page tracking

From: Tian Zheng <zhengtian10@huawei.com>

Armv9.5 introduces FEAT_HDBSS, the hardware dirty state tracking
structure: with VTCR_EL2.{HDBSS,HA,HD} set, the hardware promotes
writable-clean stage-2 descriptors to writable-dirty without a
permission fault and records the dirtied IPAs into an in-memory
per-vCPU buffer. This series uses it for hardware-assisted dirty page
tracking during live migration: instead of write-protecting guest
pages and taking a fault on every first write, KVM leaves the pages
writable-clean and drains the buffer into the dirty bitmap (or dirty
ring) on every VM exit.

The series builds on Leonardo Bras' three-state stage-2 encoding RFC
[1], which splits the traditional RW descriptor encoding into
Non-writable / Writable-clean / Writable-dirty states, with DBM
carrying writability and S2AP[1] carrying dirtiness. Patches 1-4 pick
up that series (with adaptations noted below), patch 5 closes the host
folio accounting gap that hardware dirty management opens, patches
6-10 add the HDBSS buffer plumbing, and patch 11 replaces the RFC's
HAFDBS enablement and our earlier HDBSS enable/disable hooks with a
single derived hardware dirty mode.

Adaptations to the picked-up patches:

  - Patch 1 keeps reading writability from S2AP[1] alone in
    walk_nested_s2_pgd(): KVM hides HAFDBS.DBM from L1 (its visible
    ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1.HAFDBS is limited to AF-only), so bit 51 is RES0
    from L1's perspective and could be misread as writability. The
    union reading can return once nested HAFDBS emulation exists.

  - Patch 2 additionally gates the host folio account on PROT_W while
    the dirty bitmap keeps the PROT_DIRTY gate, following the sashiko
    review of the RFC [2]: a writable-clean mapping can be
    hardware-promoted to writable-dirty without any VM exit, so the
    folio must be marked speculatively at fault-in time or reclaim can
    discard written guest data.

  - Patch 11 is a rework of "Enable HAFDBS for guests not on
    migration" [3]: the RFC's kvm_set_hafdbs() and our earlier
    HDBSS enable/disable hooks both flip VTCR_EL2 bits from
    kvm_arch_commit_memory_region() in opposite directions. The rework
    merges them into kvm_arch_update_hw_dirty_mode(), a pure function
    of the static capabilities and the number of logging memslots, and
    additionally pairs HD with HA, flushes the VMID after each mode
    switch, and derives writable-clean decisions from the live HD
    state instead of the static capability.

The FEAT_HDBSS buffer registers (HDBSSBR_EL2, HDBSSPROD_EL2) are
EL2-only and programmed from host context, so the feature is
restricted to VHE systems. Nested guests are excluded: shadow stage-2
MMUs do not carry the HDBSS bits, and L2 tracking stays on the classic
fault path.

Patch 12 adds a VM ioctl to configure the buffer size in dirty-bitmap
mode, and patch 13 documents it. In dirty-ring mode the buffer size is
pinned to PAGE_SIZE: the ring reserves room for a full buffer flush,
so a larger buffer would only shrink the soft limit and force more
frequent userspace drains.

The series is also the vehicle for hardware dirty tracking on systems
without FEAT_HDBSS: the derived mode falls back to the classic
write-protect fault mechanism there, while still using HAFDBS outside
of migration so that only pages actually written to are dirty when the
next migration starts.

