---
v4 -> v5:
 - Rebase onto Leonardo Bras' three-state stage-2 encoding RFC [1]
   (patches 1-4 picked up with adaptations, replacing our two-state
   DBM enablement); drop our KVM_REQ_HDBSS_RELOAD_VTCR in favour of
   the RFC's KVM_REQ_RELOAD_STAGE2.
 - Replace the standalone HAFDBS enablement and our HDBSS
   enable/disable hooks with a single derived hardware dirty mode
   (patch 11): fixes the opposite-direction VTCR_EL2 writes, the
   vcpu-setup race (HD as a pure derived value), sets HA alongside
   HD, flushes the VMID on mode switches, and derives writable-clean
   decisions from the live HD state (VGA-style always-logged slots no
   longer degrade to a fault per page on non-logged slots).
 - Split the host folio account from the dirty-bitmap account
   (patch 2) and harvest the stage-2 dirty bit on unmap and
   write-protect (patch 5), closing a silent data loss on reclaim
   found by the sashiko review of the encoding RFC.
 - Keep reading L1 writability from S2AP[1] only in
   walk_nested_s2_pgd() (patch 1): bit 51 is RES0 from L1's
   perspective until nested HAFDBS emulation exists.

v3 -> v4:
 - Fix HDBSS buffer size vs buddy order encoding mismatch on 16KB/
   64KB pages, which over-released on free.
 - Drain the HDBSS buffer before the exit reason is handled in
   kvm_arch_vcpu_ioctl_run(), closing the dirty-ring feedback loop.
 - Fix kvm_share_hyp() error path leaking the hyp mapping, and free
   the HDBSS buffer in the vCPU destroy path.
 - Drop the forced eager-page-splitting default; write-protect now
   strips DBM from block entries so the first write faults and
   triggers the split in both lazy and eager modes.

v2 -> v3:
 - Move to the dirty-bitmap-first design with the dirty ring reserve
   wired through kvm_cpu_dirty_log_size().
 - Pin the buffer size to PAGE_SIZE in dirty-ring mode via the new
   kvm_arch_dirty_ring_size_updated() hook.

v1 -> v2:
 - Restrict the capability to VHE systems (buffer registers are
   EL2-only and programmed from host context).
 - Keep buffers allocated for the vCPU lifetime, decoupled from the
   enable/disable lifecycle (PML-style), fixing the UAF found by
   sashiko in v1.

